  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe GTE perpetuals protocol tracks Open Interest through two distinct but interdependent accounting systems.\n\n**Position Open Interest System**\nThe protocol maintains Position OI in `MarketMetadata` which tracks the aggregate of all actual user positions:\n\n```solidity\nstruct MarketMetadata {\n    uint256 longOI;   // Total long positions across all users\n    uint256 shortOI;  // Total short positions across all users\n    // ... other fields\n}\n```\n\nThis system correctly updates when positions change through the `updateOI` function:\n\n```solidity\nfunction updateOI(bytes32 asset, OIDelta memory oiDelta) internal {\n    MarketMetadata storage metadata = StorageLib.loadMarketMetadata(asset);\n\n    if (oiDelta.long > 0) metadata.longOI += oiDelta.long.abs();\n    else if (oiDelta.long < 0) metadata.longOI -= oiDelta.long.abs();\n\n    if (oiDelta.short > 0) metadata.shortOI += oiDelta.short.abs();\n    else if (oiDelta.short < 0) metadata.shortOI -= oiDelta.short.abs();\n}\n```\n\n**Book Open Interest System**\n\nSeparately, the protocol tracks Book OI in each order book's metadata, representing the total exposure from pending orders:\n\n```solidity\nstruct BookMetadata {\n    uint256 baseOI;   // Total base amount in pending orders\n    uint256 quoteOI;  // Total quote amount in pending orders\n    // ... other fields\n}\n```\n\nBook OI increases when orders are posted to the book and should decrease when orders are filled or removed. The two OI systems should theoretically represent the same underlying economic reality from different perspectives.\n\nThe issue occurs in the `_matchIncomingOrder` function within the order matching process. When matching against reduce-only orders, the system calls `_boundReduceOnlyOrder` to ensure the order does not exceed the user's current position:\n\n```solidity\nfunction _matchIncomingOrder(\n    Book storage ds,\n    Order storage matchedOrder,\n    Order memory incomingOrder,\n    bool baseDenominated\n) internal returns (__TradeData__ memory tradeData) {\n    address matchedOwner = matchedOrder.owner;\n\n    if (incomingOrder.owner == matchedOwner) {\n        _removeUnfillableOrder(ds, matchedOrder);\n        return tradeData;\n    }\n\n    if (matchedOrder.reduceOnly) _boundReduceOnlyOrder(ds, matchedOrder);\n\n    tradeData = ds.getTradedAmounts({\n        makerBase: matchedOrder.amount,\n        takerAmount: incomingOrder.amount,\n        price: matchedOrder.price,\n        baseDenominated: baseDenominated\n    });\n\n    if (tradeData.baseTraded == 0) return tradeData;\n\n    // ... rest of matching logic\n}\n```\n\nThe problematic `_boundReduceOnlyOrder` function modifies Book OI immediately upon discovering an oversized reduce-only order:\n\n```solidity\nfunction _boundReduceOnlyOrder(Book storage ds, Order storage order) internal {\n    uint256 positionAmount = StorageLib.loadMarket(ds.config.asset).position[order.owner][order.subaccount].amount;\n\n    if (positionAmount < order.amount) {\n        uint256 reduceAmount = order.amount - positionAmount;\n\n        order.amount = positionAmount;\n\n        if (order.side == Side.BUY) ds.metadata.quoteOI -= reduceAmount.fullMulDiv(order.price, 1e18);\n        else ds.metadata.baseOI -= reduceAmount;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhen `_boundReduceOnlyOrder` executes, it permanently modifies the Book OI by reducing `ds.metadata.baseOI` or `ds.metadata.quoteOI`. This reduction represents the \"excess\" portion of the reduce-only order that exceeds the user's current position. However, this creates accounting corruption because the function removes this excess from Book OI regardless of whether any actual trade occurs. Even when trades execute successfully, the Book OI experiences additional reduction beyond what the actual trade should cause.\n\nThe problem compounds because `_boundReduceOnlyOrder` incorrectly treats the excess portion of reduce-only orders as representing actual exposure that should be immediately removed from accounting. In reality, this excess represents a mismatch between stale order size and current position size - not real exposure that needs immediate removal. This faulty assumption causes Book OI to become understated relative to the actual market exposure tracked by Position OI.\n\nFunding rate calculations, which depend on accurate Open Interest measurements to maintain equilibrium between perpetual and spot prices, become systematically incorrect.\n\n## Recommended Mitigation Steps\n\nMove the OI adjustment logic after successful trade validation rather than performing it immediately when bounding the order. The system should only adjust Open Interest when an actual trade occurs, not during the preparation phase.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\nimport {PerpManagerTestBase} from \"../perps/PerpManagerTestBase.sol\";\nimport {Side, TiF} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Enums.sol\";\nimport {PlaceOrderArgs, AmendLimitOrderArgs} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Structs.sol\";\nimport {Order} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {Position} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Position.sol\";\n\ncontract PoCPerps is PerpManagerTestBase {\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        // Step 1: rite opens a 10 ETH long position\n        _placeTrade(ETH, rite, jb, 4000e18, 10e18, Side.BUY, 0);\n        \n        // Step 2: rite places a reduce-only sell order for 8 ETH (valid at this point)\n        vm.startPrank(rite);\n        PlaceOrderArgs memory reduceOrder = PlaceOrderArgs({\n            subaccount: 0,\n            asset: ETH,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitPrice: 4100e18, // Higher price so it goes on book\n            amount: 8e18,\n            baseDenominated: true,\n            tif: TiF.MOC,\n            expiryTime: 0,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            reduceOnly: true\n        });\n        uint256 orderId = perpManager.placeOrder(rite, reduceOrder).orderId;\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        // Step 3: rite partially closes position, reducing it to 5 ETH\n        _placeTrade(ETH, rite, nate, 4000e18, 5e18, Side.SELL, 0);\n        \n        // Now rite has 5 ETH position but 8 ETH reduce-only order on book\n        \n        // Step 4: Record OI before the corrupting event\n        (uint256 beforeOI,) = perpManager.getOpenInterestBook(ETH);\n        \n        // Step 5: Someone tries to buy against the stale reduce-only order\n        // This triggers _boundReduceOnlyOrder which corrupts OI\n        vm.startPrank(moses);\n        usdc.mint(moses, 100000e18);\n        usdc.approve(address(perpManager), type(uint256).max);\n        perpManager.deposit(moses, 100000e18);\n        \n        PlaceOrderArgs memory buyOrder = PlaceOrderArgs({\n            subaccount: 0,\n            asset: ETH,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitPrice: 4100e18,\n            amount: 1e18, // Small amount to trigger matching\n            baseDenominated: true,\n            tif: TiF.IOC,\n            expiryTime: 0,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            reduceOnly: false\n        });\n        perpManager.placeOrder(moses, buyOrder);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        // Step 6: Check OI after the trade\n        (uint256 afterOI,) = perpManager.getOpenInterestBook(ETH);\n        \n        // The reduce-only order was bounded from 8 to 5 ETH (3 ETH excess)\n        // This 3 ETH was permanently deducted from baseOI due to the bug\n        uint256 corruption = beforeOI - afterOI;\n        \n        // Verify corruption occurred\n        assertTrue(corruption >= 3e18, \"Expected 3 ETH OI corruption from bounded reduce-only order\");\n        \n        // Check if the order was actually bounded\n        Order memory order = perpManager.getLimitOrder(ETH, orderId);\n        if (order.amount > 0) {\n            // Order still exists, should be bounded to position size (5 ETH)\n            assertLe(order.amount, 5e18, \"Order should be bounded to position size\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
